# The Erlang shell

## About this notebook

The Erlang shell is the read-eval-print loop (REPL) of Erlang: the module `shell` of stdlib. In a terminal, `beam.com` starts it with no arguments. This notebook starts the Erlang shell and IEx, the shell of Elixir, in the VM of this Livebook, each with a terminal in the output of a cell.

The same shell of beam.com also runs:

* as a web page of its own, with its VM in the page: [`repl/`](https://sntran.github.io/BEAM.com/repl/) of this site. It is a much smaller download than Livebook;
* on Deno Deploy, with the VM on the server: <https://beam.one.deno.net>. There, the shell is a restricted shell, because all the visitors share the VM;
* on Cloudflare Workers and natively, from the same source ([`examples/worker`](../../examples/worker)).

Here, the shell is not restricted. It runs in the VM of this tab, as the cells do.

## How a shell talks to a terminal

A shell reads its input and writes its output through its group leader, with the I/O protocol of Erlang: the messages `{io_request, From, ReplyAs, Request}` and `{io_reply, ReplyAs, Reply}`. `Repl.Session` is a process that is the group leader of a shell:

* A read (`get_until` for the Erlang shell, `get_line` for IEx) waits for a line from the terminal. The session sends the prompt to the terminal.
* A write (`put_chars`) goes to the terminal.
* An interrupt stops the evaluator: the process that evaluates an expression for the shell. The shell prints the exit and starts a new evaluator.

```elixir
defmodule Repl.Session do
  @moduledoc """
  A shell (the Erlang shell or IEx) with this process as its group leader:
  the process gives the shell the lines of the terminal, and sends the
  owner {:repl, session, {:output, text}} and {:repl, session, {:prompt, text}}.
  """

  def start(kind) do
    owner = self()
    spawn(fn -> init(owner, kind) end)
  end

  def input(session, line), do: send(session, {:input, String.to_charlist(line) ++ ~c"\n"})
  def interrupt(session), do: send(session, :interrupt)

  defp init(owner, kind) do
    Process.monitor(owner)
    Process.group_leader(self(), self())

    shell =
      case kind do
        :erlang ->
          :shell.start(false, false)

        :elixir ->
          {:ok, _} = Application.ensure_all_started(:iex)
          spawn(fn -> IEx.Server.run([]) end)
      end

    Process.monitor(shell)
    loop(%{owner: owner, shell: shell, buffer: [], pending: nil})
  end

  defp loop(s) do
    receive do
      {:io_request, from, reply_as, request} -> loop(request(from, reply_as, request, s))
      {:input, line} -> loop(take(%{s | buffer: s.buffer ++ line}))
      :interrupt -> loop(interrupt_evaluation(s))
      {:DOWN, _, :process, pid, _} when pid == s.owner -> Process.exit(s.shell, :kill)
      {:DOWN, _, :process, pid, _} when pid == s.shell -> send(s.owner, {:repl, self(), :down})
      _ -> loop(s)
    end
  end

  # The requests of the I/O protocol that read wait for a line.
  defp request(from, reply_as, {:get_until, enc, prompt, m, f, xs}, s),
    do: wait(from, reply_as, {:until, enc, prompt, m, f, xs, []}, s)

  defp request(from, reply_as, {:get_until, prompt, m, f, xs}, s),
    do: wait(from, reply_as, {:until, :latin1, prompt, m, f, xs, []}, s)

  defp request(from, reply_as, {:get_line, enc, prompt}, s),
    do: wait(from, reply_as, {:line, enc, prompt}, s)

  defp request(from, reply_as, {:get_line, prompt}, s),
    do: wait(from, reply_as, {:line, :latin1, prompt}, s)

  defp request(from, reply_as, request, s) do
    send(from, {:io_reply, reply_as, simple(request, s)})
    s
  end

  defp simple({:put_chars, enc, chars}, s) do
    send(s.owner, {:repl, self(), {:output, :unicode.characters_to_binary(chars, enc)}})
    :ok
  end

  defp simple({:put_chars, enc, m, f, a}, s), do: simple({:put_chars, enc, apply(m, f, a)}, s)
  defp simple({:put_chars, chars}, s), do: simple({:put_chars, :latin1, chars}, s)
  defp simple({:put_chars, m, f, a}, s), do: simple({:put_chars, :latin1, m, f, a}, s)

  defp simple({:requests, requests}, s),
    do: Enum.reduce_while(requests, :ok, fn r, _ -> r |> simple(s) |> then(&if(&1 == :ok, do: {:cont, :ok}, else: {:halt, &1})) end)

  defp simple(:getopts, _s), do: [binary: false, encoding: :unicode, echo: false]
  defp simple({:setopts, _}, _s), do: :ok
  defp simple({:get_geometry, :columns}, _s), do: 80
  defp simple({:get_geometry, :rows}, _s), do: 24
  defp simple(_, _s), do: {:error, :request}

  defp wait(from, reply_as, read, %{pending: nil} = s), do: take(%{s | pending: {from, reply_as, read, true}})

  defp wait(from, reply_as, _read, s) do
    send(from, {:io_reply, reply_as, {:error, :busy}})
    s
  end

  # Gives the input to the read that waits, or sends the prompt.
  defp take(%{pending: nil} = s), do: s

  defp take(%{pending: {from, reply_as, read, first}} = s) do
    case read(read, s.buffer) do
      {:done, reply, rest} ->
        send(from, {:io_reply, reply_as, reply})
        %{s | pending: nil, buffer: rest}

      {:more, read1, rest} ->
        send(s.owner, {:repl, self(), {:prompt, prompt(read, first)}})
        %{s | pending: {from, reply_as, read1, false}, buffer: rest}
    end
  end

  defp read({:until, enc, prompt, m, f, xs, cont}, buffer) when buffer != [] do
    case apply(m, f, [cont, buffer | xs]) do
      {:done, result, rest} -> {:done, result, if(rest == :eof, do: [], else: rest)}
      {:more, cont} -> {:more, {:until, enc, prompt, m, f, xs, cont}, []}
    end
  end

  defp read({:until, _, _, _, _, _, _} = read, []), do: {:more, read, []}

  defp read({:line, enc, _prompt} = read, buffer) do
    case :string.split(buffer, ~c"\n") do
      [line, rest] -> {:done, encode(line ++ ~c"\n", enc), rest}
      _ -> {:more, read, buffer}
    end
  end

  defp encode(chars, :unicode), do: :unicode.characters_to_binary(chars) |> :unicode.characters_to_list()
  defp encode(chars, _), do: chars

  # The prompt of a read, and the prompt of the next lines of an expression.
  defp prompt(read, first) do
    prompt = read |> elem(2) |> :io_lib.format_prompt(:unicode) |> :unicode.characters_to_list()

    if first or elem(read, 0) == :line,
      do: :unicode.characters_to_binary(prompt),
      else: :unicode.characters_to_binary(:shell.default_multiline_prompt(prompt))
  end

  # The shell runs an expression in an evaluator: for the Erlang shell a
  # process linked to it, for IEx a process of IEx.Evaluator. The shell
  # prints the exit, and starts a new evaluator.
  defp interrupt_evaluation(s) do
    {:links, links} = Process.info(s.shell, :links)

    iex =
      for pid <- Process.list(),
          Process.info(pid, :group_leader) == {:group_leader, self()},
          {:dictionary, d} = Process.info(pid, :dictionary),
          match?({IEx.Evaluator, _, _}, d[:"$initial_call"]),
          do: pid

    for pid <- links ++ iex, is_pid(pid), pid != self(), do: Process.exit(pid, :kill)
    s
  end
end
```

`Repl.Terminal` is the terminal: a `Kino.JS.Live` with a text area and a line for input. The JavaScript runs in your browser, and the Elixir part runs in the VM.

```elixir
defmodule Repl.Terminal do
  @moduledoc "A terminal in the output of a cell, for a shell of Repl.Session."
  use Kino.JS
  use Kino.JS.Live

  # The text that the terminal keeps for a new view of the output.
  @keep 65_536

  def new(kind), do: Kino.JS.Live.new(__MODULE__, kind)

  @impl true
  def init(kind, ctx) do
    {:ok, assign(ctx, session: Repl.Session.start(kind), text: "", prompt: "")}
  end

  @impl true
  def handle_connect(ctx) do
    {:ok, %{text: ctx.assigns.text, prompt: ctx.assigns.prompt}, ctx}
  end

  @impl true
  def handle_event("input", line, ctx) do
    Repl.Session.input(ctx.assigns.session, line)
    {:noreply, keep(assign(ctx, prompt: ""), ctx.assigns.prompt <> line <> "\n")}
  end

  def handle_event("interrupt", _, ctx) do
    Repl.Session.interrupt(ctx.assigns.session)
    {:noreply, ctx}
  end

  @impl true
  def handle_info({:repl, _, {:output, text}}, ctx) do
    broadcast_event(ctx, "output", text)
    {:noreply, keep(ctx, text)}
  end

  def handle_info({:repl, _, {:prompt, prompt}}, ctx) do
    broadcast_event(ctx, "prompt", prompt)
    {:noreply, assign(ctx, prompt: prompt)}
  end

  def handle_info({:repl, _, :down}, ctx) do
    broadcast_event(ctx, "output", "\n** the shell stopped: run the cell again\n")
    {:noreply, ctx}
  end

  defp keep(ctx, text) do
    all = ctx.assigns.text <> text
    size = byte_size(all)
    all = if size > @keep, do: binary_part(all, size - @keep, @keep), else: all
    assign(ctx, text: all)
  end

  asset "main.js" do
    """
    export function init(ctx, data) {
      ctx.root.innerHTML = `
        <style>
          .term { background: #1c1917; color: #e7e5e4; border-radius: 8px; padding: 10px 12px;
                  height: 360px; overflow-y: auto; cursor: text;
                  font: 13px/1.45 ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, Consolas, monospace; }
          .term pre { margin: 0; white-space: pre-wrap; word-break: break-word; font: inherit; }
          .term .line { display: flex; }
          .term .prompt { white-space: pre; color: #c4b5fd; }
          .term input { flex: 1; min-width: 0; background: transparent; border: 0; outline: 0;
                        color: inherit; font: inherit; padding: 0; }
          .keys { font: 12px system-ui, sans-serif; color: #78716c; margin-top: 4px; }
        </style>
        <div class="term"><pre></pre><div class="line"><span class="prompt"></span><input
          spellcheck="false" autocomplete="off" aria-label="The line for the shell"></div></div>
        <div class="keys">Enter: send the line · ↑ and ↓: the lines before · Ctrl+C: stop the evaluation</div>`;
      const term = ctx.root.querySelector(".term");
      const out = term.querySelector("pre");
      const prompt = term.querySelector(".prompt");
      const input = term.querySelector("input");
      const lines = [];
      let at = 0;
      const bottom = () => { term.scrollTop = term.scrollHeight; };
      // The colors of ANSI escape codes (IEx uses them): 30 to 37, and 90 to 97.
      const colors = ["#a8a29e", "#f87171", "#4ade80", "#facc15", "#60a5fa", "#c084fc", "#22d3ee", "#e7e5e4"];
      let color = null, bold = false;
      const append = (text) => {
        for (const part of text.split(/(\\x1b\\[[0-9;]*m)/)) {
          const codes = /^\\x1b\\[([0-9;]*)m$/.exec(part);
          if (codes) {
            for (const c of (codes[1] || "0").split(";").map(Number)) {
              if (c === 0) { color = null; bold = false; }
              else if (c === 1) bold = true;
              else if (c === 39) color = null;
              else if (c >= 30 && c <= 37) color = colors[c - 30];
              else if (c >= 90 && c <= 97) color = colors[c - 90];
            }
          } else if (part) {
            const span = document.createElement("span");
            span.textContent = part;
            if (color) span.style.color = color;
            if (bold) span.style.fontWeight = "600";
            out.append(span);
          }
        }
        bottom();
      };
      append(data.text);
      prompt.textContent = data.prompt;
      bottom();
      term.addEventListener("click", () => { if (!getSelection().toString()) input.focus(); });
      input.addEventListener("keydown", (e) => {
        if (e.key === "Enter") {
          const line = input.value;
          append(prompt.textContent + line + "\\n");
          prompt.textContent = "";
          if (line.trim() && lines.at(-1) !== line) lines.push(line);
          at = lines.length;
          input.value = "";
          ctx.pushEvent("input", line);
        } else if (e.key === "ArrowUp" && at > 0) {
          input.value = lines[--at];
          e.preventDefault();
        } else if (e.key === "ArrowDown" && at < lines.length) {
          input.value = lines[++at] ?? "";
          e.preventDefault();
        } else if (e.key === "c" && e.ctrlKey && input.selectionStart === input.selectionEnd) {
          append(prompt.textContent + input.value + "^C\\n");
          input.value = "";
          ctx.pushEvent("interrupt", null);
          e.preventDefault();
        }
      });
      ctx.handleEvent("output", append);
      ctx.handleEvent("prompt", (text) => { prompt.textContent = text; bottom(); });
    }
    """
  end
end
```

## The Erlang shell

Click in the terminal, and type an expression that ends with a full stop. Try these lines:

* `X = lists:seq(1, 10).`
* `[Y * Y || Y <- X, Y rem 2 =:= 0].`
* `b().` shows the variables, and `f().` removes them.
* `Pid = spawn(fun() -> receive M -> io:format("got ~p~n", [M]) end end), Pid ! hello.`
* `erlang:system_info(system_architecture).`
* `timer:sleep(infinity).`, then <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>C</kbd>.

```elixir
Repl.Terminal.new(:erlang)
```

## IEx

IEx is the shell of Elixir. Here an interrupt starts IEx again, with no variables.

```elixir
Repl.Terminal.new(:elixir)
```

